pen race with no incumbent on either side after the term-limited president", "No polls, expert ratings, or market prices published yet", "Local Democratic turbulence from the governor's primary upset, so far limited to state politics"], "rationale": "Rhode Island's strong Democratic presidential lean and the absence of any incumbency advantage point to a Democratic win of roughly eight points. With no polls or expert ratings available, partisanship alone drives the forecast, which keeps the uncertainty band wide.", "watch": "The first credible general-election polls or expert ratings, and any sign that local anti-incumbent anger or a national environment shift is moving Rhode Island's presidential vote.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island remains one of the most reliably Democratic states in presidential politics, and this open race carries no incumbent to upend that pattern. With the term-limited president leaving the ballot, neither party gains the advantages of incumbency, and the state's roughly eight-point Democratic partisan lean is the clearest signal available. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices have been published for this race, so the forecast rests on partisanship alone.\n\nThe expected margin is about eight points Democratic, with wide uncertainty reflecting the absence of polling. Local politics show some strain, as Democrats ousted their own governor in a primary amid frustration over a bridge, but presidential voting in Rhode Island has historically tracked national partisanship more than state drama. The race rates Lean Democratic, with a Republican win possible but requiring a substantial national shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8249, "margin": 8.0